When to Use This Document

Use this document when:
• You are lending money and need collateral
• A borrower offers property as security
• You are taking a private or business loan
• A lender requests asset protection
• You want written collateral terms
• You need stronger repayment assurance

What is Included?

A complete Security Agreement usually includes:
• Borrower and lender names
• Loan or debt amount
• Description of collateral property
• Location of collateral
• Borrower promises and warranties
• Default terms
• Lender rights after default
• Governing law clause
• Signatures of parties

What Counts as Collateral?

A Security Agreement may use:
• Vehicles
• Business equipment
• Machinery
• Electronics
• Jewelry
• Inventory
• Artwork or collectibles
• Other personal property of value

Real Property vs Personal Property

Use a Security Agreement for personal property.
For land, buildings, or homes, a mortgage or deed of trust may be more appropriate.

